Power Automate + Dynamic SharePoint Lists

We remove the expensive Copilot "Chat" trigger, fully automate it in the background, and fix the hardcoded business rules.

How it works:
A user simply drops the raw file into a SharePoint folder (or the system receives it via email). A Power Automate Cloud Flow wakes up automatically. It reads the business rules (the Matrix) dynamically from a SharePoint List (which Darin’s team can edit via a simple UI). It then runs lightweight Excel scripts to format the pivots and auto-drafts the emails to stakeholders.

Pros: cost effective, 
Moving the Matrix out of code and into a SharePoint List means BU can update assignment rules, time-to-market

Cons:
If the source vendor changes the column names in the raw report, the script will still break and require a quick developer fix.

UiPath
How it works:
A UiPath unattended bot is scheduled to run every morning. It can actually log into the external vendor portal (e.g., MSP, Black Knight, etc.), download the raw file itself, process the  data tables in memory, generate the Excel output, and distribute the emails.

Pros:
End-to-End Automation: Eliminates even the "file drop" step. The bot goes and gets the data itself.

Cons:
Overkill for just formatting Excel: If we already get the file delivered easily, spinning up UiPath just to sort rows and make Pivot Tables is computationally expensive.

Python + Applied ML & LLM
How it works:
The raw data flows directly into a cloud data environment (like Azure Data Lake). Instead of running  Excel macros, we use Python (Pandas) to  process large number of rows.
We integrate an LLM (Azure OpenAI) to read any unstructured notes or comments in the raw data to categorize  exceptions

The Output: Instead of sending static Excel pivots, the data feeds a real-time Power BI Dashboard, and the AI drafts a natural-language executive summary email.
Cons: Setting up infra
